# allowed-signers for zcl.promotion_receipt.v1 — see docs/PROMOTION_RECEIPTS.md
#
# THIS FILE IS INTENTIONALLY EMPTY OF KEYS. The evidence-signing identity is the
# owner's decision, and publishing "this specific key is the evidence authority"
# is part of that decision, so no key was chosen on the owner's behalf. Until a
# key is added here the receipt ledger has zero records, and
# `tools/scripts/promotion_receipt.sh verify` reports that honestly rather than
# implying a promotion happened.
#
# It must NOT be a login or push key. That key's job is authenticating pushes;
# making it the evidence authority would mean anyone who can push can mint
# receipts, and rotating it for an ordinary git-access reason would silently
# invalidate the evidence chain. Generate a key whose only job is signing
# evidence.
#
# One-time owner setup (full version in docs/PROMOTION_RECEIPTS.md,
# "Owner setup"):
#
#   ssh-keygen -t ed25519 -C 'zclassic23 promotion receipts' \
#       -f ~/.ssh/zcl-promotion-receipt
#   printf '%s %s\n' <principal> \
#       "$(cut -d' ' -f1-2 ~/.ssh/zcl-promotion-receipt.pub)" \
#       >> platform/deploy/promotion-signers
#   ZCL_RECEIPT_KEY=~/.ssh/zcl-promotion-receipt \
#       tools/scripts/promotion_receipt.sh init
#   git add platform/deploy/promotion-signers platform/deploy/promotion-receipts.jsonl && git commit
#
# Format (ssh-keygen(1), ALLOWED SIGNERS): one line per identity permitted to
# sign a record in platform/deploy/promotion-receipts.jsonl —
#
#   <principal> <keytype> <base64-public-key>
#
# e.g.  promotions@example.invalid ssh-ed25519 AAAAC3NzaC1lZDI1NTE5AAAA...
#
# Only PUBLIC keys ever belong here. Nothing in this file is a secret and
# nothing in it can sign anything; it exists so a third party who does not trust
# whoever handed them this repository can still check every receipt offline:
#
#   tools/scripts/promotion_receipt.sh verify
#
# That command reads only this file and the ledger — no private key, no network,
# no cooperation from the author.
#
# ONE signing identity, on purpose. Key rotation is deliberately not built here:
# adding a second line is a reviewed commit, which is exactly the audit trail a
# rotation needs. A receipt signed by a key later removed from this file stops
# verifying — the honest outcome, since the whole claim of a receipt is "this
# identity vouched for this promotion".
